WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.640
What happened to the villain at the end of
"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness?

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.760
And what's up with that post-credits sequence?

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.900
These are the confusing moments in "Doctor
Strange 2" explained.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.680
"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness"
opens with America Chavez and a variant of

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.100
Doctor Strange running from a fiery beast
in a mysterious location.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.690
It's an action-packed way to start the movie
off, and as a result, it can be a bit difficult

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.710
your first time through to fully grasp what's
going on.

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:33.140
With the information revealed later in the
film, however, the film's opening starts to

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.730
make a lot more sense.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:39.100
Presumably, America encountered this ponytailed
version of Doctor Strange during her journey

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.390
through the multiverse, which she initially
undertook in search of her missing parents.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:48.059
At some point around the time of her meeting
him America starts being pursued by various

00:00:48.059 --> 00:00:49.059
monsters.

00:00:49.059 --> 00:00:52.390
She and Strange manage to figure out that
they're trying to take her powers, but they

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:55.890
don't quite solve the mystery of who's behind
the attacks.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.520
It's only revealed later that Wanda Maximoff
is the one pulling the strings, as she needs

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.830
America's universe-hopping powers to find
her children once again.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:09.200
How exactly Wanda is able to reach across
dimensions to summon beasts of such immense

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.641
powers isn't directly explained, but given
how powerful she's shown to be with the Darkhold

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:18.799
guiding her Chaos Magic, it's not too much
of a stretch to guess.

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:22.950
Of course, in the end, Scarlet Witch quits
with the monsters and decides to do the job

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.579
herself.

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:29.380
For any MCU fans who missed "WandaVision"
on Disney+, Scarlet Witch's transition from

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.200
Avenger to murderous villain might seem a
bit abrupt.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.429
Even for those who did see the miniseries,
her turn to the dark side is far more severe

00:01:37.429 --> 00:01:39.729
than anything glimpsed previously.

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:44.520
In "Multiverse of Madness," Wanda's evil ambitions
are revealed when she's asked by Doctor Strange

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.000
to help America.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.460
Little does he know that she's the one endangering
the poor girl in the first place.

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.549
"I made mistakes and people were hurt."

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:57.819
Essentially, Wanda's obsessive use of the
Darkhold since the end of "WandaVision" has

00:01:57.819 --> 00:02:03.509
poisoned her grief over time and turned it
into a violent need for control at any cost.

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:07.950
The film later reveals that her personality
has practically been split in two between

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.219
her original self and her corrupted Scarlet
Witch persona.

00:02:11.219 --> 00:02:15.311
It might seem like a bit of a jump, but that's
just the power of the Darkhold on display.

00:02:15.311 --> 00:02:18.980
"That's the book of the damned."

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.430
There are some other interesting things about
this particular scene, however.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:27.569
For instance, Wanda creates an entire elaborate
illusion of a thriving orchard to fool Strange

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:29.969
into thinking she's left her magic behind
her.

00:02:29.969 --> 00:02:34.560
He only notices the deception when she says
America's name, which she should have no way

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:35.560
of knowing.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:40.980
It's a baffling slip-up for such a powerful
and cunning character, and it raises the question:

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.579
Was the mistake an instance of the real Wanda's
personality shining through?

00:02:45.579 --> 00:02:51.219
After arriving on Earth-838, Doctor Strange
and America Chavez encounter a scanner that

00:02:51.219 --> 00:02:54.519
recalls and displays important memories.

00:02:54.519 --> 00:02:59.249
Stephen's memory scan involves his relationship
with Christine Palmer, and America's shows

00:02:59.249 --> 00:03:04.780
a tragic moment from her childhood — the
first time her portal-opening powers manifested,

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:08.080
accidentally rocketing her mothers into an
unknown dimension.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.439
In the film, the memory is mostly used to
build a bit more of an emotional foundation

00:03:12.439 --> 00:03:17.180
for America, establishing both her grief and
her drive to find her parents.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:20.939
However, the context surrounding her upbringing
isn't really touched on.

00:03:20.939 --> 00:03:25.969
In the comics, America is born in a dimension
called the Utopian Parallel — a world that

00:03:25.969 --> 00:03:30.519
exists beyond the reaches of the multiverse
and the regular timestream.

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:34.530
During a potential cataclysm that had nothing
to do with America herself, her moms made

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.099
a noble sacrifice to help protect their home.

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:42.470
Obviously, the MCU is already diverging from
that version of the story in some big ways,

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:46.459
but from what's glimpsed of America's old
home in the movie, it seems like a lot about

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:48.359
her origin may be the same.

00:03:48.359 --> 00:03:53.279
The Utopian Parallel's existence outside the
multiverse could explain why America doesn't

00:03:53.279 --> 00:03:55.609
dream or have any other variants.

00:03:55.609 --> 00:04:00.160
Even more interesting is the fact that, in
the comics, the Utopian Parallel is sustained

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.810
by the power of the Demiurge, an all-powerful
being who happens to be the final form of

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:08.000
Wanda Maximoff's son Billy, AKA Wiccan.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.629
It seems that America's connection to the
Scarlet Witch could be just beginning.

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:18.280
"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness"
spends a lot of time on Earth-838, a reality

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:23.120
in which the planet is secretly guided by
the powerful Illuminati and Thanos was defeated

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.590
with the mystical Book of Vishanti.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.620
There are a lot of little details and Easter
eggs sprinkled throughout the alternate dimension

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.270
that are easy to miss, but they provide a
lot of important context to the state of that

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:35.740
particular universe.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:41.180
While 838 isn't a major designation in the
Marvel Comics multiverse, the world seen in

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.940
"Multiverse of Madness" pulls a lot of details
from previously inked storylines.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:49.180
For instance, when Christine Palmer tells
Stephen and America that she works for the

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:54.250
Baxter Foundation, it's an immediate clue
that the Fantastic Four exist in her universe.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:59.161
The Baxter Building has long been the headquarters
of the iconic Marvel superhero team, and in

00:04:59.161 --> 00:05:04.879
the 2015 Fox film adaptation, the Baxter Foundation
is the research group that Reed Richards,

00:05:04.879 --> 00:05:07.950
Sue Storm, and the rest of the group work
for.

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:12.700
It seems unlikely that any major details from
that particular movie will be brought into

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.220
the MCU, but the similarity is still worth
noting.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.180
"Say that again?"

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:23.569
Given that Black Bolt and Professor Charles
Xavier are also present on Earth-838, it stands

00:05:23.569 --> 00:05:27.150
to reason that mutants and the Inhumans are
both present there.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:32.800
In many ways, these details make 838 feel
closer to the Earth-616 of the comics than

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.090
the main 616 universe of the MCU.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:40.849
Arguably the most exciting part of "Doctor
Strange in the Multiverse of Madness" is the

00:05:40.849 --> 00:05:44.039
introduction of the Illuminati to the MCU.

00:05:44.039 --> 00:05:48.379
Simply having a group by that name in the
franchise is a big deal, as the Illuminati

00:05:48.379 --> 00:05:51.979
are important players in a number of major
comic book storylines.

00:05:51.979 --> 00:05:56.910
Even more intriguing are the specific members
of Earth-838's Illuminati, each of whom has

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.690
a unique point of origin.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:03.240
Baron Mordo seems to be a relatively simple
variant of the character we saw in the first

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.199
"Doctor Strange," but the rest of the team
have more complicated backgrounds.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:11.500
Captain Carter is an alternate version of
Captain America first glimpsed in the MCU

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.099
in "Marvel's What If...?"

00:06:13.099 --> 00:06:14.099
Season 1.

00:06:14.099 --> 00:06:19.050
In that story, Peggy Carter ends up taking
the super-soldier serum instead of Steve Rogers

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.940
due to complications during the procedure.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:26.590
Anson Mount returns as the Inhuman Black Bolt,
though he seems to be a different version

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:30.460
than the one we last saw in the short-lived
ABC "Inhumans" show.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:36.580
The 838 version of Captain Marvel isn't Carol
Danvers, but rather her 616 wingmate Maria

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:37.580
Rambeau.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:42.830
Patrick Stewart reprises his role as Professor
X from the non-MCU "X-Men" movies, but his

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:47.930
golden chair and presentation are closer to
the version seen in "X-Men: The Animated Series."

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:50.020
"We should tell him the truth."

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:56.000
Last on the list is John Krasinski's Mr. Fantastic,
who, while not part of the franchise previously,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.479
has been a popular fancast for years.

00:06:58.479 --> 00:07:02.990
The whole group also employs a version of
Ultron that seems markedly less sinister than

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.200
the one from Earth-616.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.789
As a whole, the Illuminati in "Multiverse
of Madness" serve largely the same purpose

00:07:09.789 --> 00:07:14.550
as their equivalent team in the comics — guiding
the world in secret toward a future that they

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.840
deem to be optimal.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:21.360
After her original copy of the Darkhold is
destroyed, Wanda forces Wong to take her to

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.480
Mount Wundagore, the source of the ancient
text.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:29.379
Wong briefly explains that the mountain was
once inhabited by a demon called Chthon who

00:07:29.379 --> 00:07:31.699
carved his dark spells into the rock.

00:07:31.699 --> 00:07:36.400
When Wanda arrives, she finds what seems to
be a shrine erected to the Scarlet Witch and

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.699
a group of Cthulhu-esque monsters who bow
at her feet.

00:07:39.699 --> 00:07:44.050
In the comics, Chthon is an Elder God — one
of the oldest and most powerful beings in

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:45.250
Earth's history.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:50.289
Chthon eventually departs Earth for a parallel
dimension, but before he goes, he inscribes

00:07:50.289 --> 00:07:53.770
all the dark Chaos Magic he knows within the
walls of Mount Wundagore.

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:58.330
As an infant in the comics, Wanda Maximoff
is experimented on within the bounds of the

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.659
mountain, connecting her to Chthon's dark
power.

00:08:01.659 --> 00:08:06.449
It's still a bit unclear what percentage of
Wanda's power in the MCU is due to her exposure

00:08:06.449 --> 00:08:10.469
to the Mind Stone, and how much was latent
magical ability.

00:08:10.469 --> 00:08:15.500
Until more of the Scarlet Witch prophecy is
revealed, it's hard to say for sure.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:20.350
When Scarlet Witch dreamwalks into Earth-838's
reality in pursuit of America Chavez, she

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:24.210
quickly begins ripping a bloody path through
everyone who stands in her way.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:29.200
Her body count includes the full roster of
the Illuminati, each of whom dies in brutal

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:30.200
fashion.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:35.310
The first to fall in the battle is Black Bolt,
who also arguably suffers the most grotesque

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:36.780
fate.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:38.250
"WHY?"

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:42.600
In trying to talk Wanda down, Mr. Fantastic
tells her that Black Bolt could kill her with

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:46.000
a single word from his powerful voice.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:50.250
In response, Wanda seals the hero's mouth
completely shut, causing him to panic.

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:54.880
Overcome with terror at having his mouth glued
shut, he can't contain his fear and appears

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.560
to scream in horror.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:00.330
Because that scream has nowhere to go, it
ends up blowing his own brains out from the

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:01.330
inside.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:05.660
Scarlet Witch's kills on the other Illuminati
members are similarly gruesome.

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:10.620
She slices Captain Carter in half with her
own shield, shreds Mr. Fantastic's elastic

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.640
body into pieces, and fights back against
Professor X's psychic invasion well enough

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:18.500
to kill him from within his own mind.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:23.340
The Illuminati's last attempt at stopping
Wanda comes in the form of Charles Xavier,

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:27.810
whose formidable telepathic abilities allow
him to enter her mind in search of a way to

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:29.320
end her rampage.

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:34.370
In her mind, he finds a white void occupied
by small piles of urban rubble in a clear

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:37.330
callback to the childhood memories we see
in WandaVision.

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:42.530
Beneath one of these piles, Xavier finds a
trapped Wanda — it's not made clear exactly

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:47.440
who this Wanda is; she could be the character's
original, heroic persona that's been locked

00:09:47.440 --> 00:09:53.170
away by the Darkhold's corruptive magic, or
the psyche of Earth-838 Wanda, or something

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:54.630
else entirely.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:59.450
Charles tries to free her from her prison,
but before he can finish the job, the Scarlet

00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:03.620
Witch's own mental powers find him and extinguish
his life for good.

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:06.260
How exactly Wanda kills Charles is a bit unclear.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:11.410
Her powers aren't explicitly psychic in nature,
but it's feasible that her Chaos Magic could

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:13.820
manifest in more cognitive ways.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.640
Fans of the "X-Men" movies may get some flashbacks
to the Dark Phoenix storyline, which also

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:23.620
revolves around a red-hued hero who falls
to the dark side due to a powerful alternate

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:24.620
persona.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:30.070
Xavier's attempt to save Wanda and his subsequent
death mirror the events of "X-Men: The Last

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:34.340
Stand," and it's doubtful that's a coincidence
on the part of Marvel Studios.

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:38.820
As Scarlet Witch hunts America Chavez and
Doctor Strange, they hunt for the Book of

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:44.120
Vishanti — an ancient tome of pure, good
magic that stands in opposition to the evil

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:45.840
of the Darkhold.

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:49.690
America nearly claims the book in the opening
sequence of the movie, but she's forced to

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:52.780
bail out into Earth-616 instead.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:58.250
Later on, she, Stephen, and a variant of Christine
Palmer end up finding the Book of Vishanti,

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:02.490
but just as they're about to tap into its
power, Scarlet Witch destroys it.

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:06.870
For something that's said to be so immensely
important, the Book of Vishanti certainly

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.580
doesn't get to do much in "Doctor Strange
2."

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:14.240
In the comics, the tome is crafted by three
deities called the Vishanti, one of whom is

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:19.350
Agamotto — the same figure who forges Doctor
Strange's Eye of Agamotto.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:24.270
Not all of that lore has been officially canonized
in the MCU, but a few things about the film

00:11:24.270 --> 00:11:26.650
version of the book are made clear.

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:31.230
First and foremost, it's implied that there's
only one Book of Vishanti, as Christine claims

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:35.450
that the dimension where they find it is a
sort of nexus point between other parts of

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:36.660
the multiverse.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:41.010
That would make the book fundamentally different
from the Darkhold, which has numerous copies

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.200
across dimensions.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:47.881
After Scarlet Witch seemingly destroys the
Book of Vishanti, Doctor Strange and Christine

00:11:47.881 --> 00:11:52.070
fall through one of America's portals into
a post-apocalyptic wasteland.

00:11:52.070 --> 00:11:56.240
They deduce that the realm they've entered
is the result of an incursion — the cataclysmic

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:00.560
collision of multiple universes — and the
only survivor they manage to find is another

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:02.770
variant of Strange himself.

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:07.450
This new sorcerer proves to be quite sinister,
however, leading to a magical showdown between

00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:08.960
the two doppelgangers.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:13.660
The sequence can feel a bit confusing because
it admittedly employs a lot of awfully convenient

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:14.660
coincidences.

00:12:14.660 --> 00:12:19.140
How did that version of the Darkhold happen
to be possessed by another Doctor Strange?

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:23.530
How did they manage to find that Strange so
quickly and easily in what's essentially an

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:24.800
empty void?

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:28.920
The film doesn't really provide satisfying
answers to these questions, preferring more

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.050
of a deus ex machina approach.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:35.550
Though the evil Doctor Strange in "Multiverse
of Madness" is a different variant, he shares

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:38.550
some similarities with the one seen in "Marvel's
What If...?"

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:39.550
Season 1.

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:44.600
In that story, a version of the doctor becomes
obsessed with saving Christine's life, ultimately

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:47.340
leading him to destroy his entire universe.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:48.520
Knowledge of the "What If...?"

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:52.590
episode sheds a bit of light on the events
of "Multiverse of Madness," as it shows how

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:58.250
most versions of the character can easily
fall down dark holes of unhealthy fixation.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:13:03.000
After defeating his evil doppelganger, Stephen
takes his evil twin's copy of the Darkhold

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:07.860
and uses it to dreamwalk back into his home
dimension, using the corpse of the Doctor

00:13:07.860 --> 00:13:11.840
Strange variant brought there with America
Chavez at the beginning of the film.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:16.600
The doctor explains that dreamwalking allows
the caster to inhabit a dead body, but such

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:21.460
a desperate move comes with its own unique
set of consequences — condemnation by the

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:22.760
"souls of the damned."

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:27.600
As soon as Strange begins manipulating his
zombified form, an army of ghouls appear and

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.720
try to drag him ... well, somewhere.

00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:35.510
Presumably, the dead rise to confront Strange
because he's essentially practicing necromancy.

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:39.310
Scarlet Witch receives no such visit from
the damned when she dreamwalks into a living

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:43.850
variant of herself, so it stands to reason
that the ghosts are only bothered by someone

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:45.790
spitting in death's eye.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:49.960
Things get a bit more complicated, however,
when Christine urges Stephen to use his mystical

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:53.700
powers to control the souls of the damned,
which he does.

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:57.770
Whether this is an ability that's always been
accessible to him or something made possible

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:00.540
by the Darkhold is left unclear.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:04.740
Most likely, it's a combination of the two
— something that only a powerful sorcerer

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:08.180
could achieve through the use of dark magic.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:12.221
From start to finish of "Doctor Strange in
the Multiverse of Madness," Wanda Maximoff

00:14:12.221 --> 00:14:17.170
lives up to her potential as one of the most
powerful beings in the entire MCU.

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:22.110
She lays waste to Kamar-Taj, uses the sorcerers'
own containment spells against them, spreads

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:26.970
her consciousness across multiple realities,
and calls an army of monsters and demons to

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:28.080
do her bidding.

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.200
In the end, the only one strong enough to
defeat the Scarlet Witch is Wanda herself.

00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:36.980
"Your power exceeds that of the Sorcerer Supreme."

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:41.750
America punches Wanda through a portal back
to Earth-838, where she comes face-to-face

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:46.710
with the variant she previously possessed
and her two children, Tommy and Billy.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:51.660
Seeing the looks of terror on her sons' faces
breaks something within Wanda, stopping her

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:54.200
bloody rampage dead in its tracks.

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:59.590
She then returns to Earth-616 and proceeds
to bring down the entirety of Mount Wundagore,

00:14:59.590 --> 00:15:04.550
burying the ancient dark magic within and
destroying every universe's version of the

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:05.590
Darkhold.

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:09.660
Wanda's about-face happens pretty quickly,
but it makes sense given what's established

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:11.070
earlier in the film.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:14.860
It's made clear when Charles Xavier enters
her mind that the Scarlet Witch persona has

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:17.670
basically imprisoned Wanda within her own
mind.

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:21.820
Presumably, the sight of her terrified children
is enough to break Wanda free.

00:15:21.820 --> 00:15:26.700
Because the Scarlet Witch persona might quickly
be able to regain power, Wanda wastes no time

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:30.400
in seemingly destroying herself and the Darkhold.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:34.990
After defeating Wanda, saving the world, and
returning home, Doctor Strange tries to get

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:37.360
back to something resembling a normal life.

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:42.100
That plan is upended, however, in the final
scene before the first round of credits, in

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:45.140
which Doctor Strange collapses in the middle
of the street.

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:49.730
The last shot shows a third eye open in his
forehead, mirroring the one worn by his sinister

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:51.840
doppelganger earlier in the film.

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:56.460
Considering that Strange's evil Variant has
his own third eye as a result of using the

00:15:56.460 --> 00:16:00.350
Darkhold, it seems that our heroic Strange
might have been corrupted during his use as

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:01.390
well.

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:04.510
"Open your eye!"

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:09.710
"Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of Madness"
has a brief but significant post-credits scene.

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:13.590
While walking down the street, Strange is
accosted by a purple-clad woman played by

00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:18.840
Charlize Theron, who claims that he's responsible
for causing a multiversal incursion.

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:23.600
She demands that he comes with her to fix
it, and he readily agrees, opening his third

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:28.430
eye and following her through a portal that
she slashes open with a mystical blade.

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:33.140
The credits identify Theron's character as
Clea— a name that fans of the comics will

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:34.450
instantly recognize.

00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:38.820
In the original Marvel stories, Clea is a
resident of the Dark Dimension who fights

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:43.590
a constant battle against the evil of Dormammu,
who is also her uncle.

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:48.410
Because the ancient being is also one of Doctor
Strange's primary foes, he and Clea become

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:49.410
allies.

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:54.010
They even get married later on in the comics,
though their union eventually meets a tragic

00:16:54.010 --> 00:16:55.010
end.

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:59.321
If Clea and Stephen's MCU relationship is
at all like it is in the comics, the doctor

00:16:59.321 --> 00:17:01.410
has some big things coming his way.

